{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a367d46c5e9c1e83","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9013-E001\n3\ttitle: FIPS 140-3 crypto remediation for KServe serving images\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n5\tcomponent: KServe\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 0\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 0\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 1\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 5\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# FIPS 140-3 crypto remediation for KServe serving images\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tMake every KServe component image (control plane and data plane) run correctly on\n34\tFIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters using validated cryptographic modules, with no\n35\tnon-validated crypto in the request path. This epic also delivers the KServe\n36\tportion of the FIPS regression enforcement and the inputs to the compliance\n37\tevidence package.\n38\t\n39\t## Scope\n40\t\n41\t- Audit all KServe images for non-validated cryptographic library usage and\n42\t  produce a per-image module inventory (libraries, versions, linkage, validation\n43\t  status).\n44\t- Remediate each flagged image to link only validated crypto modules (library\n45\t  swaps, rebuilds, base-image changes as needed) without changing functional\n46\t  behavior.\n47\t- Verify the remediated images on FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters in both\n48\t  supported deployment modes (serverless and raw deployment).\n49\t- Author and verify the portion of the CI FIPS regression gating check that\n50\t  covers KServe builds: a gating check that fails any KServe build reintroducing\n51\t  non-validated crypto usage.\n52\t- Build the remediated images through the existing build pipeline (KServe is\n53\t  already onboarded) so that signed build attestations are produced and\n54\t  retrievable for the compliance evidence package.\n55\t- Hand off the module inventory, build attestations, and FIPS test results for\n56\t  aggregation into the compliance evidence package.\n57\t\n58\t## Acceptance Criteria\n59\t\n60\t- A module inventory exists for every KServe image, enumerating each crypto\n61\t  library, its version, and its validation status.\n62\t- Scanning the built KServe images confirms no non-validated crypto modules are\n63\t  linked in the request path.\n64\t- Remediated images pass functional verification on a FIPS-enabled OpenShift\n65\t  cluster in both serverless and raw deployment modes.\n66\t- The CI FIPS regression check gates KServe builds and is verified to fail a\n67\t  deliberately non-compliant test build that reintroduces non-validated crypto\n68\t  usage.\n69\t- Remediated images build green in the existing build pipeline and their signed\n70\t  attestations are retrievable for the evidence package.\n71\t\n72\t## HLR Traceability\n73\t\n74\t- **HLR-1 (P0)** — KServe serving images run on FIPS-enabled clusters using\n75\t  validated crypto modules.\n76\t- **HLR-3 (P1)** — authors and verifies the CI FIPS regression gating check\n77\t  covering KServe builds. Bundled with the P0 remediation: the check shares the\n78\t  build surfaces, image inventories, and scan tooling of the remediation it\n79\t  enforces, so it is not separately schedulable — a standalone enforcement epic\n80\t  would re-audit the same builds.\n81\t\n82\t## Risks / Notes\n83\t\n84\t- The audit determines which specific images and libraries need work, but not\n85\t  the approach (audit → remediate → verify → enforce); the epic proceeds the\n86\t  same way regardless of the audit outcome.\n87\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-to